<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Boltway.OAuth.Primitives\Boltway.OAuth.Primitives.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      For JwksKeySource: JsonWebKeySet to parse a key set, SecurityKey to hand the parsed keys back
      in the type ProtectedResourceOptions.SigningKeySource takes.

      This is the only reason this project references IdentityModel, and the alternative was worse in
      both directions. Handing back raw bytes would put the parse in whichever package called it,
      which is two implementations of "which members of a JWKS are signing keys" the day a second
      caller appears. Putting the fetch in Boltway.OAuth.Tokens instead would mean Tokens
      referencing this project — and Tokens is the one assembly both halves of the product compile
      against, so every resource server would acquire an HTTP transport whether it fetches anything
      or not.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      This is the seventeenth package, and it was the one nobody could see. Directory.Build.props
      said sixteen packages moved together, which is exactly how many projects declared IsPackable;
      this one packed anyway, because packing is what the SDK does with a library unless told
      otherwise. It was right about the files and wrong about the feed for the two publishes
      that followed — 2026-08-19, and v0.3.0 itself, which is the one that noticed.

      True is what it already was. Saying so changes no output — it makes the output legible from
      here, which is what StructuralRuleTests.Every_project_says_whether_it_packs now requires of
      every project in this repository.
    -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <Description>
      The one outbound HTTP client allowed to fetch a URL the server does not control: absolute https
      only, RFC 6890 special-use addresses refused, redirects refused, response bytes capped and
      timeouts applied. Also carries JwksKeySource, which points a resource server's verification keys
      at an issuer's published key set.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Boltway.OAuth.Net.Tests" />

    <!--
      For SafeHttpFetcherOptions.SslOptionsForTests / UpstreamEndpointClientOptions.SslOptionsForTests,
      and nothing else. The federation suite stands up a real Kestrel identity provider on loopback
      with a certificate it generates, and drives the whole sign-in through the *real* guarded
      transport against it. Without this grant the only reachable outcome is Blocked, which is how
      the original SSRF suite ended up never completing a single fetch and proving nothing.

      The grant widens what a test assembly can see. It does not widen what a customer can: the
      property is internal and not settable from configuration, so no deployment can reach it.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Boltway.AuthorizationServer.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
